# Engine and observation audit for the coordination proposal

Read-only source review, 2026-09-29. File identities/excerpts are retained in this
directory. This is not a new engine-parity execution or a learner result.

## Execution and dependence

`engine_rs/src/lib.rs`:

- 1459–1499: own worker actions, market, town consumption, crop decay, daily
  maintenance/reset, step increment and terminal cash outcome. Default episode
  length 720 produces 719 transitions under the inspected stopping condition.
- 1546–1579: count all submitted PLANT demands before worker execution; a crop
  exceeding initial seeds has every request converted to PASS. Count failed-site
  requests too. A prefix simulator cannot simply deduct successfully used seeds.
- 1588–1625: farmer then hands in ordinal order; earlier tile/inventory effects
  are visible to later workers. There is no free worker-order permutation.
- 3142–3155: cardinal movement obeys bounds, not occupancy/locked-tile rules.
- 3166–3187: DROP at shed access deposits in inventory insertion order, discards
  overflow. 3190–3214: PICKUP fills from current shed stock, omitted amount one;
  worker carrying capacity is not checked.
- 3217–3257: PLACE animal into a compatible empty structure first; otherwise
  deposit to shed up to room, retaining excess. Animal branch ignores quantity.
- 3260 onwards: shed transfers occur before locked-tile guard. PLANT requires
  empty tile at execution. WATER, HARVEST, FERTILIZE, DIG, BUILD, FEED, CARE and
  COLLECT_FERTILIZER update shared tiles and the acting worker's inventory.
- 3800–3842: HIRE cost is multiplier times Fibonacci(hires_today); successful
  hires change cash/count and spawn at least-occupied shed access. 3845 onwards:
  land unlock order NE/SW/SE and escalating prices.
- 3869–3931: SELL uses shed only; price-one sale pays without adding market
  inventory. Product/animal buys use shed room, seed buys use separate storage.
  Market inventory is a price input, not a nonnegative supply admission rule.
- 3959–4140: market positions run in order across players; HIRE/LAND before
  merchandise in a position; per-unit quotes for both players are computed before
  either commit. Orders may partially fill; subsequent quotes/cash are uncertain
  without rival submissions. EMPTY consumes a position, STOP terminates.
- 4204–4329, 4431–4528: maintenance and production, automatic deposits with
  overflow, reset worker positions/inventories, remove hired hands, random weeds
  and shop selection. A last-hour hire cannot act before disappearing.

Valid chains can include DIG→PLANT, one-shot HARVEST→PLANT, PLANT→WATER,
FERTILIZE→WATER, WATER→HARVEST, BUILD→animal PLACE→FEED/CARE, and ordered shed
transfer chains. Multiple workers may need the same tile. Other workers can
also undo a plan, so a selected-seed allocator is not a complete turn resolver.

All workers precede market: DROP→SELL and SELL→BUY/HIRE are possible; buying a
seed/new worker/land does not enable an earlier worker command this turn. Goods
deposited at day end cannot be sold in the already-finished market phase.

## Legal information boundary

`engine_rs/src/myolie_features.rs` contains the current two-seat 8176-float
encoding. It exposes own seeds/shed/per-worker inventories, both public tile and
worker fields, public cash/markets/shops, and clock/hiring/land/capacity suffixes.
The other seat's separate row contains private data and is prohibited input.

The encoding is not lossless: item insertion order is absent; scaled bank/stock
values become f32; some public custom market/config fields are omitted. Exact
shadow execution requires an explicitly versioned legal current-state contract,
not unrestricted access to `Game`. The selected proposal uses learned command
coordination and does not introduce a hidden-state simulator in the actor.

Even with exact own-state input and the full own program, market settlement and
future random maintenance are not exactly known. No current-observation-only
architecture can guarantee every contingent purchase fills while retaining all
useful sale-funded actions and opponent privacy.
